{"oat":{"id":"d554dbc4-f288-4acd-a23d-9408761a7fed","word":"oat","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"widely cultivated cereal grass","tags":["feminine"],"word":"aveia"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"The oat stalks made good straw.","bold_text_offsets":[[4,7]],"type":"example","id":"5151b012-043f-4514-8658-8c08cbe2c11b"},{"text":"The main forms of oat are meal and bran.","bold_text_offsets":[[18,21]],"type":"example","id":"cd8513ba-d6d6-435f-84b2-2ab09b2b1070"},{"text":"World trade in oat is increasing.","bold_text_offsets":[[15,18]],"type":"example","id":"b51f67ca-6c8f-4011-b63e-a1d3ffb2bfce"}],"wikidata":["Q12104"],"senseid":["en:Q12104"],"categories":["English terms with usage examples","English uncountable nouns"],"links":[["cereal","cereal"],["grass","grass"],["Avena sativa","Avena sativa#Translingual"]],"raw_glosses":["(uncountable) Widely cultivated cereal grass, typically Avena sativa."],"glosses":["Widely cultivated cereal grass, typically Avena sativa."],"tags":["uncountable"],"id":"437c2f61-e4bb-4457-be51-03d3dc5e46a9"},{"examples":[{"text":"The wild red oat is thought to be the ancestor of modern food oats.","bold_text_offsets":[[13,16],[62,65],[62,66]],"type":"example","id":"9e20c93a-577c-47a4-851d-a811239a7846"}],"categories":["English countable nouns","English terms with usage examples"],"links":[["Avena","Avena#Translingual"]],"raw_glosses":["(countable) Any of the numerous species, varieties, or cultivars of any of several similar grain plants in genus Avena."],"glosses":["Any of the numerous species, varieties, or cultivars of any of several similar grain plants in genus Avena."],"tags":["countable"],"id":"d55e76cc-afd2-40d0-a0b1-f542cd678900"},{"examples":[{"text":"[…]I could munch your good dry Oates. Me-thinkes I haue a great deſire to a bottle of hay: good hay, ſweete hay hath no fellow.","ref":"c. 1595–1596 (date written), William Shakespeare, “A Midsommer Nights Dreame”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies: Published According to the True Originall Copies (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act IV, scene i], page 157, column 1:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[31,36]],"id":"0245bfbb-edbc-45a5-a948-969208462327"},{"text":"The point is, except in Scotland, people eat comparatively few oats. Scotland's another story, though you'll have to decide how seriously to take it. The way the story goes is that in eastern Scotland, the unmarried plowmen didn't eat anything but oats and milk, except for an occasional potato.","ref":"1991, Cornelia M. Parkinson, Cooking with Oats: Oat Bran, Oatmeal, and More, Storey Publishing, →ISBN, page 2:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[63,67],[248,252]],"id":"c66b17d5-3350-4d94-83d2-e9915cc4a02c"}],"links":[["seed","seed"],["grain","grain"],["harvest","harvest"],["food crop","food crop"],["feed","feed#Noun"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"raw_glosses":["(usually as plural) The seeds of the oat, a grain, harvested as a food crop and for animal feed."],"glosses":["The seeds of the oat, a grain, harvested as a food crop and for animal feed."],"tags":["countable","uncountable","usually"],"raw_tags":["as plural"],"id":"432dc0c5-fc72-48e2-94ed-7cafd5a21aed"},{"links":[["musical","musical"],["pipe","pipe"]],"glosses":["A simple musical pipe made of oat-straw."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"dd758e8c-19a7-4fe3-b4ba-8ba85ca37e5e"},{"examples":[{"text":"Few of them care an oat for the niceties of the arrow sport, but for the young lords that may be on a hunt!","ref":"1994, Susan King, The Black Thorne's Rose, page 21:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[20,23]],"id":"8ef14c07-fc34-40ec-af41-4d63af88845d"}],"links":[["whit","whit"],["jot","jot"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"glosses":["The tiniest amount; a whit or jot."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"c4630994-0f07-4f79-a737-884c71bc409c"}],"related":[{"word":"bran"}],"synonyms":[]},"oath":{"id":"4030a011-3365-40d3-a755-57dcfaa5f634","word":"oath","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to pledge","word":"jurar"}],"pos":"verb","tags":null,"senses":[{"categories":["English terms with archaic senses"],"links":[["pledge","pledge"]],"raw_glosses":["(archaic) To pledge."],"glosses":["To pledge."],"tags":["archaic"],"id":"31beed7c-e676-4496-be1b-f9e3a12285ed"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"oats":{"id":"a6b0d90d-53a5-4e6c-8199-e87ab289f8f1","word":"oats","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"seeds of an oat plant","tags":["feminine"],"word":"aveia"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Oats are in the north corner of the farm.","bold_text_offsets":[[0,4]],"type":"example","id":"7d13156b-860c-4e63-aa93-b778b04568eb"},{"text":"Usually oats is last in a rotation and does not get the fertilizer that other feed grains get.","bold_text_offsets":[[8,12]],"type":"example","id":"761e05ca-d8d4-4a95-ac99-32725d557af7"}],"links":[["oat","oat"],["Avena","Avena#Translingual"],["Avena sativa","Avena sativa#Translingual"]],"glosses":["A mass of oat plants (genus Avena, especially Avena sativa)."],"categories":["English terms with usage examples"],"tags":["plural","singular"],"id":"7f35709b-d0f0-4b24-89d5-6b10b0f41ea0"},{"examples":[{"text":"Oats is a heart-healthy breakfast food.","bold_text_offsets":[[0,4]],"type":"example","id":"495cd224-25e3-49dd-a1a6-3b0354406e01"},{"text":"Oats are good for you.","bold_text_offsets":[[0,4]],"type":"example","id":"5b484758-ea84-4a16-8a1e-3597df93df61"}],"glosses":["Seeds of an oat plant, especially prepared as food."],"categories":["English terms with usage examples"],"tags":["plural","singular"],"id":"7110f5f6-8bb0-4409-9385-3dc6df03b05e"},{"examples":[{"text":"Must I beat the Bush while you catch the Game ;\nSow your wild oates,\nAnd mind not her wild notes,\nOf Alack &c.\n[…]\n[She] bid me fill, fill, fill\nHer Sack &c.\nBut twas all in Vaine\nFor I had spilt my graine\nWith a Thwack, &c.","ref":"a. 1707, George Farquhar, The Works of George Farquhar, published 1988, page 55:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[62,67]],"id":"ae5f248d-7d04-4524-a0f1-e6f2bbf6785d"},{"text":"“[…]he’s young,” he added excusingly. “And I suppose he’s just feeling his oats a bit.”\n“Well he can save his damned oats up until we get into port—like any other sailor. If I catch him making those sort of passes at any woman who happens to be making a passage with us, Mister Samuals, I’ll disrate him and send him to the fo’c’sle!”","ref":"1945, Robert Close, Love me Sailor, published 1972, page 62:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[75,79],[117,121]],"id":"c4e33fb7-bf26-4d31-9218-f43b85006438"},{"text":"Now you been foolin’ with your oats, you been messin’ with your hay, you say, “If you take me back, baby, I’ll do it the other way.”","ref":"1975, Bruce Jackson, Get Your Ass in the Water and Swim Like Me: Narrative Poetry from Black Oral Tradition, page 135:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[31,35]],"id":"d7dd8aa6-0c7f-4104-a15f-97a2903a1a34"}],"attestations":[{"date":"from 18th century","references":[]}],"categories":["English slang","English terms with quotations"],"links":[["Sexual intercourse","sexual intercourse#English"],["satisfaction","satisfaction"],["semen","semen"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"wild oats"},{"word":"seed"},{"word":"aphrodisia","source":"Thesaurus:copulation","tags":["archaic"]},{"word":"carnal knowledge","source":"Thesaurus:copulation"},{"word":"coitus","source":"Thesaurus:copulation"},{"word":"coition","source":"Thesaurus:copulation"},{"word":"commerce","source":"Thesaurus:copulation","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"commixtion","source":"Thesaurus:copulation","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"congress","source":"Thesaurus:copulation","tags":["dated"]},{"word":"conjunction","source":"Thesaurus:copulation","tags":